The code snippet of interest is in priest.c, line
509, where this part applies after naked AC:1 and
until naked AC-10:

   ( ... || !rn2(u.ublessed)))

which says throw a random integer for the number of
blessings so far received, and allow the next
blessing if that random integer is zero. The range
of the returned values will be 8 to 0 for the case
of u.ublessed being 9; up to then the donation is
guaranteed to be successful so long as the cash is
right. The first pass, that number of blessings
(presuming it is protection granted, and not number
of _times_ protection was granted, again I'm too
lazy to go look) got you from AC:10 to AC:1, so it's
9, and you have a 1 in 9 chance of being blessed
with protection for each donation until one succeeds.

The proper way the calculation is done, as every one
who does this calculation regularly for dungeon
dicing or any other probability and statistics sense
already knows, is to find the point at which the
odds of your _not_ having received a point of
protection drop below 50%.

With 90% odds of failure, that works like this:

1 donation: (8/9)
  = 0.888888888... chance of one failure in a row
2 donations:  (8/9)*(8/9)
  = 0.790123456... chance of two failures in a row
3 donations: (8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)
  = 0.702331961... chance of three failures in a row
4 donations: (8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)
  = 0.624295076... chance of four failures in a row
5 donations: (8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)
  = 0.554928957... chance of five failures in a row
6 donations: (8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)*(8/9)
  = 0.493270184... chance of six failures in a row

The tip over is between 5 and 6 donations, your
expectation of how many donations you will make to
gain that next point of protection is roughly 5.9,
_not_ 10.0. Since Byron is frittering away his
zorkmids by paying double or triple or even
quadruple for protection points, it's pretty much a
given that he isn't going to be donating past that
first point of excess protection, so that's what
he'll be expecting to see.

Similar calculations pertain for addition excess
protection points and their tipover expectations for
numbers of donations during which failure is the
more likely case. [The strange notation here is so
that I can plug this directly into Microsoft's
miserable calculator tool via cut and paste.]

Second point of excess protection, tipover is
between:

9*9*9*9*9*9/10/10/10/10/10/10=0.531441
9*9*9*9*9*9*9/10/10/10/10/10/10/10=0.4782969

or roughly 6.6 tries before success is more likely
than failure.

Third point of excess protection, tipover is
between:


10*10*10*10*10*10*10/11/11/11/11/11/11/11=0.51315811823070675574202125080136

10*10*10*10*10*10*10*10/11/11/11/11/11/11/11/11=0.46650738020973341431092840981942

or roughly 7.2 tries before success is more likely
than failure.

Fourth point of excess protection, tipover is
between:


11*11*11*11*11*11*11/12/12/12/12/12/12/12=0.54385117826038808870598994055784

11*11*11*11*11*11*11*11/12/12/12/12/12/12/12/12=0.49853024673868908131382411217802

or roughly 8.0 tries before success is more likely
than failure.

Fifth point of excess protection, tipover is
between:

12*12*12*12*12*12*12*12/13/13/13/13/13/13/13/13=0.52711229935399233296743766991215

12*12*12*12*12*12*12*12*12/13/13/13/13/13/13/13/13/13=0.48656519940368523043148092607276

or roughly 8.6 tries before success is more likely
than failure.

After each point of protection is gained, the wait
until the next one is gained begins, so the total
wait is expected to be the sum of the individual
waits:

5.9 + 6.6 + 7.2 + 8.0 + 8.6 = 36.3

expected donations for 5 excess points of
protection. Yes, that's boring as heck, but it is
sure worth preserving your zorkmids to attempt by
staying at XL:1, especially since the exercise of
doing so is good practice at running away, pet
management, and other crucial dungeon skills.

